---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { levelById, lessonFor } from '../../data/curriculum.js';

// Named in PLAIN TEXT, never linked — this is lesson 3 of 102 and both targets sit
// further along the course (invariant 9). Both the level title and the lesson title
// DERIVE: they were typed here, and a rename elsewhere would have left this sentence
// quietly naming pages that no longer exist under those names.
const lvl = (id) => { const l = levelById(id); return `${l.id} · ${l.title}`; };
const lvlLesson = (href) => lessonFor(href).label;
import LessonNav from '../../components/LessonNav.astro';
import LessonEyebrow from '../../components/LessonEyebrow.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import { shamirNote, bip85 } from '../../data/ladder.js';
import Shamir from '../../components/demos/Shamir.astro';
import '../../styles/demo.css';

// ADDED 2026-07-30. Both of these used to be callouts on the ladder page, which was
// wrong twice over: they are not rungs (neither changes how many keys sign), and a
// two-sentence callout can't carry a real trade-off. This needs a page
// explaining what those are in this section... and then, of course, on that page we
// can link out to the demonstrations."
//
// BIP-85 first, Shamir second, which is the right order: BIP-85 makes
// backups FEWER, Shamir makes one backup SPLIT. Fewer-then-split reads as a widening
// of the same question ("how many pieces should my backup be in?"), where the reverse
// reads as two unrelated tricks.
//
// It sits BEFORE choose-a-wallet on purpose, not after: Shamir needs device support
// (Trezor implements SLIP-39 natively, others vary), so a reader who cares about it
// needs to know before they spend money, not after.
//
// The explanation lives HERE, not on the demos. It briefly lived on /demos/shamir
// (earlier the same day, when the ladder merge had nowhere else to put it) — that is
// undone: the lesson explains, the demo shows, and the demo pages link back here.
const s = shamirNote;
---
<Base
  title="Beyond the ladder — BIP-85 and Shamir backup · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="Two optional refinements that aren't rungs on the ladder: BIP-85 derives many seeds from one master so you keep fewer backups, and Shamir (SLIP-39) splits one backup into shares where any few rebuild it. What each buys you, and what each costs."
  verified="2026-07-31"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <LessonEyebrow />
    <h1>Beyond the ladder — <span class="accent">two refinements that aren’t rungs</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">The ladder is about <em>how many keys sign</em>. These two are about something else entirely: <strong>how your backups are shaped</strong>. Neither adds a signer, so neither is a rung — you can bolt either onto any rung, or skip both and lose nothing important.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">You can skip this lesson</span>
      <p style="margin:0">Nothing here is required to hold Bitcoin safely, and most holders never use either. It’s here because you <em>will</em> hear both names, and it’s better to know what they are — and why you probably don’t need them — than to wonder.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>BIP-85 — one master seed, many child seeds</h2>
    <p><strong>The problem it solves:</strong> backups multiply. A wallet here, a passphrase wallet there, a multisig key somewhere else — and each one is another set of words to write down, store, and keep track of for decades.</p>
    <p set:html={bip85.note.replace(/ <a href="[^"]*">[^<]*<\/a>/, '')}></p>
    <p>
      In practice: you keep <strong>one</strong> master seed backed up properly, and derive every other wallet
      you need from it, on demand, by index — child number 1, child number 2, and so on. The children are
      ordinary seed phrases; nothing about them looks unusual, and they work in any wallet.
    </p>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="label">What it costs you</span>
      <p style="margin:0">You have concentrated everything onto one seed. That master now deserves the care you would otherwise have spread across all of them — because it is no longer <em>a</em> backup, it is <em>every</em> backup. And you must remember which child index was which, which is a new note to keep, though not a secret one.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="deeper"><strong>See it work:</strong> <a href="/demos/bip85">watch one master spawn child seeds →</a> — verified against the BIP-85 spec test vectors, on throwaway keys.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Shamir backup (SLIP-39) — one backup, split into shares</h2>
    <p><strong>The problem it solves:</strong> a single seed backup has to be in one place, and that place can burn down, flood, or be found.</p>
    <p set:html={s.whatItIs.replace(/ <a href="[^"]*">[^<]*<\/a>/, '')}></p>

    <Diagram name="shamir" caption="Any three of the five shares rebuild the seed. Any two reveal nothing at all." />

    <h3>What it’s good at</h3>
    <ul>{(s.gains ?? []).map((g) => <li set:html={g}></li>)}</ul>

    <h3>What it costs you</h3>
    <ul>{(s.costs ?? []).map((c) => <li set:html={c}></li>)}</ul>

    {s.keyRisk && (
      <div class="callout warn">
        <span class="label">{s.keyRisk.label}</span>
        <p set:html={s.keyRisk.body}></p>
      </div>
    )}

    <h3>Who should use it</h3>
    <p set:html={s.whoShould}></p>

    {/* INLINE. This lesson is the shortest of the three that reference demos
        (877 words), and Shamir is the claim readers most often disbelieve —
        that two shares reveal literally nothing. Reading that is not the same
        as dropping a share and watching the value mask itself. The component
        carries its own "never your real seed" callout, so inlining it cannot
        leave the warning behind. /demos/shamir is unchanged and still its own
        page; this is the same component, not a copy. */}
    <div class="inline-demo">
      <p class="idemo-k mono">Try it here — the same demo that lives on its own page</p>
      <Shamir />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="callout safe">
      <span class="label">Choosing between them — and whether to bother</span>
      <p style="margin:0">They pull in opposite directions. <strong>BIP-85 gives you fewer things to protect</strong> and concentrates the risk; <strong>Shamir gives you more things to protect</strong> and spreads it. If keeping track of several backups is what worries you, BIP-85 is the one to look at. If one backup sitting in one place is what worries you, Shamir is — though a later lesson’s advice applies first (<strong>{lvlLesson('/learn/back-up-your-seed')}</strong>, in {lvl('103')}): <strong>two ordinary copies in two separate places solves that same problem with no new machinery at all</strong>, and for most holders that is the better answer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <LessonNav />
</Base>
